      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Influenza A viral infection is concerned with induction of asthma. CD11c+ pulmonary antigen presenting cells (APCs) play a central role in sensitization with inhaled antigens during the acute phase of influenza A viral infection and also reside on bronchial epithelium for the long term after sensitization. To investigate the role of CD11c+ pulmonary APCs in the inhaled antigen sensitization during the acute phase of influenza A viral infection, we analyzed their function."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Mice were infected with influenza A virus and were sensitized intranasally with BSA/alum during the acute phase of influenza A viral infection. Expression of surface antigens on CD11c+ pulmonary APCs was analyzed by FACS. Cytokine production from CD11c+ pulmonary APCs, and interaction between CD11c+ pulmonary APCs and naïve CD4+ T cells was assessed by ELISA. Ability of antigen presentation by CD11c+ pulmonary APCs was measured by proliferation assay."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "BSA antigen sensitization during the acute phase of influenza A viral infection induced eosinophil recruitment into the lungs after BSA antigen challenge and moderately increased expression of MHC class II molecules on CD11c+ pulmonary APCs. The interaction between the CD11c+ pulmonary APCs and naïve CD4+ T cells secreted large amounts of IL-10."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "BSA antigen sensitization during the acute phase of influenza A viral infection enhanced IL-10 production from naïve CD4+ T cell interaction with CD11c+ pulmonary APCs. The IL-10 secretion evoked Th2 responses in the lungs with downregulation of Th1 responses and was important for the eosinophil recruitment into the lungs after BSA antigen challenge."
        }
      ]},
